// @error is not lowered yet
// `x as? T` — the trio's COALESCING member — parses and is refused at the CHECKER, so it
// reads as a type error with a remedy rather than as an emitter message.
//
// It is the one member whose RESULT type differs: `as` and `as!` both yield a `T`, while
// `as?` yields `T | null`. That is what it needs and they do not, and the rep of `T | null` is
// PER-`T` — a nullable REF for a string / struct / sub-union arm, a value NICHE for a scalar.
//
// THE LOWERING WAS BUILT AND MEASURED. A value-producing `if` (`fbIfRefNul` for the result
// blocktype, the unbox on the then arm, `ref.null` on the else) emits the right bytes. What
// stops it is one layer out: every binding and consumer classifier then has to see through
// the cast to the NULLABLE kind, exactly as `exprString`, `exprVariantIndex` and `exprUnion`
// each needed an arm for the non-nullable forms. Adding the nullable-string arm alone moved
// the failure from `bare null needs a struct-typed context` to a heap-type mismatch one layer
// further out, and the struct arm needs its own. That is the remaining work, and it is
// per-rep rather than one arm — which is why this is a refusal and not a floor with a guess
// behind it.
//
// NOTHING IS LOST MEANWHILE, and the ruling says why: `as?` DESTROYS the error value — it
// "collapses `IoError{code, msg}` into `null`", so it is the LOSSY member, not the
// load-bearing one. `as` propagates the reason and `as!` traps, and both are lowered.
type IoError = { code: i32, msg: string }
function readIt(p: string): string | IoError {
  if p == "" { return { code: 44, msg: "missing" } }
  return "hello"
}

function head(p: string): string {
  const s = readIt(p) as? string
  return "x"
}
print(1)
